      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Patients with spinal deformities and nerve compression due to spinal tuberculosis often require surgical intervention. The objective of this study was to assess the long-term therapeutic effectiveness of one-stage anterior debridement, bone grafting, and internal fixation for lower cervical tuberculosis with kyphosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Twenty-one patients with lower cervical tuberculosis and cervical kyphosis received one-stage anterior debridement, autologous iliac bone grafting, and internal plate fixation. Patients were followed-up postoperatively for at least 5 years. Outcome measures included neck pain using Visual Analogue Scale (VAS) scores, Frankel classification of spinal cord injury, and Cobb angle of cervical kyphosis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Eighteen patients had lesions in 2 vertebrae and 3 had lesions in 3 vertebrae. Cervical tuberculosis was cured in all patients. The mean preoperative VAS score for neck pain was 8 (range: 6-10), whereas the mean best postoperative VAS score was 0.6 (range: 0-3). Mean final VAS score was significantly higher than the best VAS score (2.14 vs. 0.62). Of the 13 patients with symptoms of spinal cord compression, 9 improved by 1 grade and 4 improved by 2 grades according to Frankel classification at final follow-up. The mean preoperative Cobb angle of cervical kyphosis was 29° (range: 15°-50°), whereas the mean postoperative Cobb angle at final follow-up was -1.8° (range: 2-7°)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "One-stage anterior debridement, bone grafting, and internal fixation can effectively remove lesions, decompress the nerve, reduce pain, and correct kyphosis in patients with cervical tuberculosis and associated kyphosis. Long-term postoperative outcomes were satisfactory."
        }
      ]},
